1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,800
Mutation questions in the HSC give you something

2
00:00:02,880 --> 00:00:06,800
to read, a DNA sequence, a chromosome diagram,

3
00:00:06,880 --> 00:00:08,800
a karyotype or a case.

4
00:00:09,440 --> 00:00:11,680
Every one comes down to three checks.

5
00:00:12,240 --> 00:00:14,960
What changed, base by base or gene by

6
00:00:15,040 --> 00:00:15,800
gene?

7
00:00:16,160 --> 00:00:18,480
What does it do, to the codons, the

8
00:00:18,560 --> 00:00:19,920
frame, or the genes?

9
00:00:20,400 --> 00:00:21,760
And who can inherit it?

10
00:00:22,720 --> 00:00:25,280
Here are four HSC-style questions.

11
00:00:25,680 --> 00:00:27,920
Pause and try each one, then we'll work

12
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:30,000
it through, one step at a time.

13
00:00:31,200 --> 00:00:32,320
Question one.

14
00:00:32,640 --> 00:00:34,520
Here's the template strand for the start of

15
00:00:34,560 --> 00:00:37,360
a gene, and three mutants, each with one

16
00:00:37,440 --> 00:00:38,880
base changed.

17
00:00:39,040 --> 00:00:42,560
Write the mRNA for the normal sequence.

18
00:00:42,680 --> 00:00:45,280
Then, for each mutant, name the type of

19
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:47,733
point mutation, and describe its effect on the

20
00:00:47,760 --> 00:00:48,920
polypeptide.

21
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:50,560
Pause here to answer.

22
00:01:00,967 --> 00:01:02,966
First, which strand is it?

23
00:01:03,447 --> 00:01:06,566
It's the template, so the mRNA isn't a

24
00:01:06,647 --> 00:01:09,206
copy of it, it's built by pairing with

25
00:01:09,287 --> 00:01:10,006
it.

26
00:01:10,167 --> 00:01:12,966
A pairs with U, T with A, C

27
00:01:13,047 --> 00:01:15,206
with G, and G with C.

28
00:01:15,607 --> 00:01:18,646
The strand is written 3' to 5', so

29
00:01:18,727 --> 00:01:23,846
the mRNA lines up underneath, 5' to 3'.

30
00:01:23,967 --> 00:01:26,406
T A C gives A U G, the

31
00:01:26,487 --> 00:01:27,566
start codon.

32
00:01:27,927 --> 00:01:32,406
Reading on, the chain is methionine, proline, glutamate,

33
00:01:32,527 --> 00:01:34,406
phenylalanine, glycine.

34
00:01:35,634 --> 00:01:38,473
Now compare each mutant, one triplet at a

35
00:01:38,514 --> 00:01:39,193
time.

36
00:01:39,474 --> 00:01:42,753
In mutant one, the second codon becomes C

37
00:01:42,914 --> 00:01:43,633
C U.

38
00:01:44,114 --> 00:01:46,193
That's still proline, so it's silent.

39
00:01:46,834 --> 00:01:49,873
In mutant two, the third codon becomes U

40
00:01:50,114 --> 00:01:53,313
A A, a stop codon, so it's nonsense,

41
00:01:53,394 --> 00:01:55,793
and the chain stops after two amino acids.

42
00:01:56,354 --> 00:01:59,393
In mutant three, the fourth codon becomes U

43
00:01:59,554 --> 00:02:02,593
C U, serine instead of phenylalanine.

44
00:02:02,914 --> 00:02:04,113
That's missense.

45
00:02:05,334 --> 00:02:07,813
The classic mistake is reading the template as

46
00:02:07,894 --> 00:02:10,013
if it were the mRNA.

47
00:02:10,294 --> 00:02:12,613
That turns T A C into U A

48
00:02:12,734 --> 00:02:15,573
C, tyrosine, so there's no start codon at

49
00:02:15,654 --> 00:02:16,213
all.

50
00:02:16,454 --> 00:02:19,413
A template strand is paired, not copied.

51
00:02:20,634 --> 00:02:21,513
Question two.

52
00:02:21,994 --> 00:02:25,753
In 1961, Francis Crick and his colleagues studied

53
00:02:25,834 --> 00:02:28,713
a gene in a virus that infects bacteria.

54
00:02:29,034 --> 00:02:31,513
Here's part of a normal mRNA, and a

55
00:02:31,594 --> 00:02:34,273
mutant that still made a working protein.

56
00:02:34,554 --> 00:02:37,553
Identify the two point mutations in the mutant.

57
00:02:37,834 --> 00:02:41,113
Then explain why amino acids six and seven

58
00:02:41,194 --> 00:02:43,673
are normal, even though two to five have

59
00:02:43,954 --> 00:02:44,793
changed.

60
00:02:44,954 --> 00:02:46,153
Pause here to answer.

61
00:02:56,567 --> 00:02:59,046
Four codons in a row have changed, which

62
00:02:59,127 --> 00:03:01,366
one substitution can't do.

63
00:03:01,487 --> 00:03:04,006
So line them up base by base.

64
00:03:04,087 --> 00:03:06,166
The first four bases match.

65
00:03:06,367 --> 00:03:08,566
Then the normal sequence has a C that

66
00:03:08,607 --> 00:03:11,286
the mutant doesn't, so there's a deletion in

67
00:03:11,367 --> 00:03:12,726
codon two.

68
00:03:12,887 --> 00:03:15,206
After that, the mutant's bases are the normal

69
00:03:15,287 --> 00:03:18,406
ones, shifted one place left, until just after

70
00:03:18,487 --> 00:03:21,606
codon five, where an extra A appears.

71
00:03:21,727 --> 00:03:23,046
That's an insertion.

72
00:03:24,267 --> 00:03:25,706
Now read in threes.

73
00:03:26,187 --> 00:03:29,226
The deletion shifts the frame, so codons two

74
00:03:29,307 --> 00:03:32,186
to five are regrouped, and those amino acids

75
00:03:32,267 --> 00:03:33,066
change.

76
00:03:33,307 --> 00:03:35,626
The insertion adds one base back.

77
00:03:36,027 --> 00:03:39,226
One removed, one added, so from codon six

78
00:03:39,387 --> 00:03:41,866
on, the bases fall into their normal groups

79
00:03:41,907 --> 00:03:42,906
of three again.

80
00:03:43,147 --> 00:03:45,346
That's why six and seven are normal.

81
00:03:45,627 --> 00:03:47,866
Results like this helped show that the code

82
00:03:47,947 --> 00:03:49,386
is read in threes.

83
00:03:50,600 --> 00:03:53,480
The classic mistake is saying a frameshift changes

84
00:03:53,560 --> 00:03:55,880
every amino acid to the end.

85
00:03:55,960 --> 00:03:58,520
That's true of one deletion on its own.

86
00:03:58,840 --> 00:04:01,640
Here, the insertion puts the frame back, so

87
00:04:01,720 --> 00:04:03,880
only the stretch between them changes.

88
00:04:05,100 --> 00:04:06,060
Question three.

89
00:04:06,620 --> 00:04:09,660
The diagram shows a normal chromosome, with genes

90
00:04:09,740 --> 00:04:12,460
as letters, and a mutated version of it.

91
00:04:13,100 --> 00:04:15,660
Name the mutation, and justify your answer.

92
00:04:16,300 --> 00:04:19,900
Then, one person with Turner syndrome has forty-five

93
00:04:19,980 --> 00:04:22,300
chromosomes, with only one X.

94
00:04:22,940 --> 00:04:25,820
Identify this type of mutation, and explain how

95
00:04:25,900 --> 00:04:28,620
it could arise during meiosis in one parent.

96
00:04:29,180 --> 00:04:30,540
Pause here to answer.

97
00:04:40,967 --> 00:04:42,726
Compare gene by gene.

98
00:04:43,207 --> 00:04:44,766
A is where it should be.

99
00:04:45,047 --> 00:04:49,206
Then the normal B, centromere, C, D reads

100
00:04:49,527 --> 00:04:52,206
D, C, centromere, B.

101
00:04:52,487 --> 00:04:55,126
It's the same genes on the same chromosome,

102
00:04:55,207 --> 00:04:58,006
with that stretch reversed, centromere and all.

103
00:04:58,407 --> 00:04:59,766
So it's an inversion.

104
00:05:00,247 --> 00:05:02,486
Nothing is lost or added, and nothing has

105
00:05:02,567 --> 00:05:04,086
come from another chromosome.

106
00:05:05,300 --> 00:05:06,420
Now part b.

107
00:05:06,980 --> 00:05:10,980
Forty-five chromosomes means a whole chromosome is missing,

108
00:05:11,060 --> 00:05:13,700
so this is a chromosomal mutation in number.

109
00:05:14,180 --> 00:05:16,100
It can come from nondisjunction.

110
00:05:16,580 --> 00:05:19,780
In one parent's meiosis, the sex chromosomes fail

111
00:05:19,860 --> 00:05:22,660
to separate, so one gamete gets no sex

112
00:05:22,740 --> 00:05:23,700
chromosome at all.

113
00:05:24,260 --> 00:05:27,620
If an X-carrying gamete fertilises it, the zygote

114
00:05:27,740 --> 00:05:30,580
has forty-four autosomes and one X.

115
00:05:31,800 --> 00:05:34,280
The classic mistake in part a is calling

116
00:05:34,360 --> 00:05:37,880
it a translocation, because the centromere moved.

117
00:05:37,960 --> 00:05:39,960
But every gene is still on the same

118
00:05:40,040 --> 00:05:41,320
chromosome.

119
00:05:41,360 --> 00:05:45,080
A translocation needs a different, non-matching chromosome.

120
00:05:46,300 --> 00:05:47,500
Question four.

121
00:05:47,820 --> 00:05:51,100
Belgian Blue cattle have very large muscles.

122
00:05:51,300 --> 00:05:53,420
Most carry two copies of an allele of

123
00:05:53,500 --> 00:05:56,700
the myostatin gene with eleven bases deleted from

124
00:05:56,780 --> 00:05:58,460
its coding sequence.

125
00:05:58,540 --> 00:06:02,060
Myostatin is a protein that limits muscle growth.

126
00:06:02,140 --> 00:06:05,180
Explain how deleting eleven bases can leave the

127
00:06:05,220 --> 00:06:07,900
cattle with no working myostatin.

128
00:06:08,060 --> 00:06:10,940
Then, a normal cow develops the same deletion

129
00:06:11,020 --> 00:06:12,700
in one muscle cell.

130
00:06:12,780 --> 00:06:15,660
Explain whether her calves could inherit large muscles

131
00:06:15,740 --> 00:06:16,700
from it.

132
00:06:16,860 --> 00:06:18,060
Pause here to answer.

133
00:06:28,467 --> 00:06:29,666
Start with the number.

134
00:06:30,067 --> 00:06:32,706
Eleven isn't a multiple of three, so the

135
00:06:32,787 --> 00:06:34,866
deletion shifts the reading frame.

136
00:06:35,347 --> 00:06:38,066
Every codon after it is regrouped, so the

137
00:06:38,147 --> 00:06:40,786
amino acids from there on change, and a

138
00:06:40,867 --> 00:06:43,026
stop codon soon turns up.

139
00:06:43,067 --> 00:06:45,266
The protein is cut short, and it's missing

140
00:06:45,347 --> 00:06:47,186
the part that does its job.

141
00:06:47,267 --> 00:06:50,866
With no working myostatin, nothing limits muscle growth,

142
00:06:50,947 --> 00:06:52,546
so the muscles grow larger.

143
00:06:53,767 --> 00:06:54,966
Now the cow.

144
00:06:55,207 --> 00:06:57,926
Her deletion is in one muscle cell, so

145
00:06:58,007 --> 00:06:59,526
it's a somatic mutation.

146
00:07:00,007 --> 00:07:03,286
It's copied only into that cell's descendants, not

147
00:07:03,367 --> 00:07:04,806
into her eggs.

148
00:07:04,887 --> 00:07:07,046
So her calves can't inherit it from this

149
00:07:07,367 --> 00:07:07,766
mutation.

150
00:07:08,247 --> 00:07:10,006
To pass it on, it would have to

151
00:07:10,127 --> 00:07:11,526
be in her germ line.

152
00:07:12,734 --> 00:07:15,293
The classic mistake in part a is saying

153
00:07:15,454 --> 00:07:17,773
eleven amino acids are lost.

154
00:07:17,894 --> 00:07:20,853
Eleven bases aren't eleven codons, and they aren't

155
00:07:20,894 --> 00:07:23,453
a whole number of codons either, so the

156
00:07:23,574 --> 00:07:24,333
frame shifts.

157
00:07:25,534 --> 00:07:28,533
So here's how to start any mutation question.

158
00:07:28,814 --> 00:07:31,533
For a sequence, find the strand, write the

159
00:07:31,694 --> 00:07:35,853
mRNA, then compare codon by codon.

160
00:07:36,254 --> 00:07:38,893
If several codons in a row differ, align

161
00:07:38,974 --> 00:07:41,533
base by base, and count: is the frame

162
00:07:41,614 --> 00:07:43,533
shifted, and is it put back?

163
00:07:44,014 --> 00:07:46,813
For a chromosome, compare gene by gene.

164
00:07:47,134 --> 00:07:50,173
And for any mutation, ask where it is,

165
00:07:50,254 --> 00:07:51,773
somatic or germ line.

166
00:07:52,414 --> 00:07:56,413
Next, how meiosis and fertilisation vary offspring.

167
00:07:57,634 --> 00:07:59,073
Here are the key notes.

168
00:07:59,314 --> 00:08:01,233
Pause the video if you need more time

169
00:08:01,314 --> 00:08:02,113
to copy them.
